const fq=[['Is mobility the same as stretching?','Not exactly. Stretching is mostly static lengthening. Mobility is controlled movement through a useful range, often with strength at the end of that range, which transfers better to calisthenics skills.'],['Should I do mobility before or after training?','A short dynamic warm-up before training, then deeper mobility after or on rest days. Long static stretches right before heavy strength work can leave you temporarily weaker.'],['How often should I train mobility for calisthenics?','Three to five short sessions a week is enough for most people. Daily is fine if the work stays easy and pain-free.'],['Will mobility help my handstand or pistol squat?','Yes. Shoulder and wrist mobility support handstands and frog stands; hip and ankle mobility support deep squats, cossacks and pistols. Pair the matching flow with your skill practice.'],['Do I need equipment for calisthenics mobility?','Most drills need only the floor. A pull-up bar unlocks dead hangs and skin-the-cat, which are excellent for shoulders and spine.']];
